# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
from nums import *

H1("3. Modelling")
P("Two solution routes are built. The first is an exact solution of the linear layered problem in the Laplace domain, inverted numerically, and it is used as the reference. The second is a finite element method with convolution quadrature in time, which handles the full nonlinear problem and is the source of all the parametric results. The preferred route of the task, a Legendre wavelet collocation method with a Caputo operational matrix, was not implemented; the reasons are given at the end of Section 3.5.")
H2("3.1 Laplace-domain solution of the linear layered problem")
P("Let a hat denote the Laplace transform in time, with $s$ the transform variable, and let a prime denote $\\mathrm{d}/\\mathrm{d}x$. The reference solution requires constant conductivity ($\\chi_i=0$) and constant perfusion ($\\omega_i=1$). With the quiescent initial state, the transform of Eq. (@ndmot@) and of Eq. (@ndenergy@) is, for each layer,")
EQ(r"\hat\rho\,s^{2}\hat u=\sigma_s\left(a_8\hat u''-a_9\hat\theta'\right)", "lt1")
EQ(r"\kappa_e\hat\theta''=\left(\hat CT_s+a_6L_p\right)\hat\theta+a_5T_s\hat u'-L_q\hat Q", "lt2")
P("where the layer index is dropped. For the MGL and fractional MGL models the symbols are given by the following relations, and the Caputo derivative has become the power $s^{1+\\alpha}$:")
EQ(r"\sigma_s=1+\tau_1s,\quad T_s=s+\tau_0^{\alpha}s^{1+\alpha},\quad \kappa_e=\hat\kappa,\quad L_p=L_q=1", "mglsym")
P("The MGT model of the source study is used for comparison. Its energy equation is $\\partial_t(\\kappa\\theta''+a_3v'')=(1+\\tau_q\\partial_t)(\\dots)$ with $v_t=\\theta$, and the classical elastic law, so that")
EQ(r"\sigma_s=1,\quad T_s=s\left(1+\tau_qs\right),\quad \kappa_e=\hat\kappa+\frac{a_3}{s},\quad L_p=L_q=1+\tau_qs", "mgtsym")
P("The Pennes limit corresponds to $\\tau_0=0$ and $\\tau_1=0$ in Eq. (@mglsym@). The laser transform is $\\hat Q=a_7g\\hat f$ with the pulse transform")
EQ(r"\hat f(s)=\frac{1-e^{-s\tau_p}}{s}", "fhat")
P("Introducing the transformed strain $e=\\hat u'$ and the transformed temperature $\\hat\\theta$ as the state $y=(e,\\hat\\theta)^{\\mathrm{T}}$, differentiating Eq. (@lt1@) once and eliminating $\\hat\\theta''$ with Eq. (@lt2@) gives a constant-coefficient system with a forcing term:")
EQ(r"e''=m\,e+r\,\hat\theta'',\qquad m=\frac{\hat\rho s^{2}}{\sigma_sa_8},\qquad r=\frac{a_9}{a_8}", "lt3")
EQ(r"y''=\mathbf{M}\,y-F(x),\qquad \mathbf{M}=\begin{pmatrix} m+rb & rA\\ b & A\end{pmatrix}".replace(r"\begin{pmatrix} m+rb & rA\\ b & A\end{pmatrix}", r"\left[\,m+rb,\ rA;\ b,\ A\,\right]"), "lt4")
P("where the matrix is written row by row, and where $A=(\\hat CT_s+a_6L_p)/\\kappa_e$ and $b=a_5T_s/\\kappa_e$. The forcing is $F=(r,1)^{\\mathrm{T}}L_q\\hat Q/\\kappa_e$. The eigenvalues $\\mu_{1,2}$ of $\\mathbf{M}$ give the two wave numbers $q_k$ of the layer, and the eigenvectors follow from the second row:")
EQ(r"\mu_{1,2}=\frac{\mathrm{tr}\,\mathbf{M}}{2}\pm\sqrt{\frac{(\mathrm{tr}\,\mathbf{M})^{2}}{4}-\det\mathbf{M}},\qquad q_k=\sqrt{\mu_k},\ \ \mathrm{Re}\,q_k>0", "eig")
EQ(r"v_k=\left(\mu_k-M_{22},\ M_{21}\right)^{\mathrm{T}}", "evec")
P("When the thermoelastic coupling $a_5$ is small, which is the case here with $a_{51}=" + sc(a1["a5"], 3) + "$, the two eigenvectors are nearly the coordinate directions and the wave numbers are close to $\\sqrt{m}$ (elastic) and $\\sqrt{A}$ (thermal). The eigenvector formula is therefore replaced by the first-row formula $(M_{12},\\mu_k-M_{11})^{\\mathrm{T}}$ whenever the latter has the larger norm, which avoids a loss of precision. The displacement is not an independent unknown. Eq. (@lt1@) determines it algebraically from the strain and the temperature,")
EQ(r"\hat u=\frac{\sigma_s\left(a_8e'-a_9\hat\theta'\right)}{\hat\rho\,s^{2}}", "ures")
P("and the transformed stress is $\\hat\\sigma=\\sigma_s(a_8e-a_9\\hat\\theta)$. To avoid exponentials that overflow, each layer $[x_a,x_b]$ uses a mode decaying to the right from $x_a$ and a mode decaying to the left from $x_b$,")
EQ(r"y(x)=\sum_{k=1}^{2}\left[c_k^{+}e^{-q_k(x-x_a)}+c_k^{-}e^{q_k(x-x_b)}\right]v_k+y_p(x)", "modes")
P("The laser has two exponential terms, $e^{-\\gamma_jx}$ with $\\gamma_j=l_j/\\delta^{*}$ and weights $B_1$ and $-B_2$, and for each of them the particular solution $y_p=c_je^{-\\gamma_jx}$ follows from a $2\\times2$ solve,")
EQ(r"\left(\gamma_j^{2}\mathbf{I}-\mathbf{M}\right)c_j=-F_j,\qquad F_j=\frac{L_qa_7\hat f\,B_j}{\kappa_e}\,(r,1)^{\mathrm{T}}", "part")
P("The eight amplitudes $c_k^{\\pm}$ of the two layers are fixed by four boundary conditions and four interface conditions. The transformed boundary conditions are")
EQ(r"\hat\sigma_1(0,s)=0,\qquad \hat\theta_1(0,s)=\frac{\theta_0}{s},\qquad \hat u_2(1,s)=0,\qquad \hat\theta_2(1,s)=0", "ltbc")
EQ(r"\left[\hat u\right]_h=\left[\hat\sigma\right]_h=\left[\hat\theta\right]_h=\left[\kappa_e\hat\theta'\right]_h=0", "ltif")
P("These eight linear equations form a complex $8\\times8$ system, which is solved by Gaussian elimination with partial pivoting for each value of $s$:")
EQ(r"\mathbf{A}(s)\,\mathbf{c}(s)=\mathbf{b}(s)", "sys8")
P("For the temperature ramp test of Section 4.2 the boundary data are changed to $\\theta_0(1-e^{-t/\\tau_b})$, whose transform is $\\theta_0[1/s-1/(s+1/\\tau_b)]$. The time-domain solution is obtained with the Durbin formula, in which the inverse transform is approximated by a Fourier series with period $2T$,")
EQ(r"f(t)\approx\frac{e^{ct}}{T}\left[\frac12\mathrm{Re}\,\hat f(c)+\sum_{k=1}^{N}\mathrm{Re}\left\{\hat f\!\left(c+\frac{ik\pi}{T}\right)e^{ik\pi t/T}\right\}\right],\quad T=2t,\ ct=6", "durbin")
P("and the slowly convergent tail of the partial sums is accelerated with the Wynn epsilon algorithm,")
EQ(r"\varepsilon_{k+1}^{(j)}=\varepsilon_{k-1}^{(j+1)}+\frac{1}{\varepsilon_{k}^{(j+1)}-\varepsilon_{k}^{(j)}},\qquad \varepsilon_0^{(j)}=\mathcal{S}_j,\quad \varepsilon_{-1}^{(j)}=0", "wynn")
P("with $\\mathcal{S}_j$ the partial sums. The accuracy of the inversion was checked by changing $N$, the Durbin parameters $(c,T)$ and the number of accelerated terms. For $\\alpha=0.6$ the largest change of any field over 19 points and three times is " + sct(max(EX["lapabs_a0.6_t0.1"][0], EX["lapabs_a0.6_t0.3"][0], EX["lapabs_a0.6_t0.5"][0]), 1) + ", which means that the reference is accurate to about ten digits. "
  "For $\\alpha=1$ the solution has jump discontinuities at the thermal and elastic fronts and the series converges slowly near them; the change is " + sct(EX["lapabs_a1_t0.1"][0], 1) + " at $t=0.1$, " + sct(EX["lapabs_a1_t0.3"][0], 1) + " at $t=0.3$ and " + g(EX["lapabs_a1_t0.5"][0], 2) + " at $t=0.5$ at the worst point, with a median change below " + sct(EX["lapabs_a1_t0.5"][1], 1) + ". The integer-order reference is therefore reliable away from fronts and at early times and it is less reliable near fronts at later times.")
P("Two remarks on the reference solution complete this subsection. The inversion needs $s\\neq0$, which the Durbin contour guarantees, and the transformed solution has a branch point at $s=0$ for $\\alpha<1$ because of the non-integer power. The principal branch of the power is used and the contour lies to the right of the branch cut, so that no special treatment is required. The second remark concerns the two wave numbers. When the thermal and elastic wave numbers are close for some $s$, the eigenvectors could become ill-conditioned, but the weak coupling keeps them well separated, and the first-row and second-row alternatives of the eigenvector formula cover the remaining cases. No loss of precision of the $8\\times8$ solution was observed, and the agreement with the finite element solution to the level of four to five digits for $\\alpha=0.6$ in the smooth ramp test supports this.")
H2("3.2 Weak form and finite element discretisation")
P("The slab is divided into $n_1$ elements in the tumour and $n_2$ elements in the normal tissue, with a node at $x=h$ and a uniform spacing in each layer. The unknowns are $u$ and $\\theta$ at the nodes, with linear shape functions $N_j$. Multiplying the motion equation by a test function $w$ with $w(1)=0$ and integrating by parts uses the stress-free condition at $x=0$ and the continuity of $\\Sigma$ at the interface as natural conditions,")
EQ(r"\int_0^1\hat\rho\,\partial_t^2u\;w\,\mathrm{d}x+\int_0^1\left(1+\tau_1\partial_t\right)\left(a_8\partial_xu-a_9\theta\right)\partial_xw\,\mathrm{d}x=0", "weak1")
P("The energy equation is multiplied by a test function $v$ with $v(0)=v(1)=0$. The Kirchhoff form means that the flux $\\hat\\kappa\\phi'=\\hat\\kappa(1+\\chi\\theta)\\theta'$ is continuous at the interface as a natural condition, and no derivative of a coefficient appears:")
EQ(r"\int_0^1\hat\kappa(1+\chi\theta)\,\partial_x\theta\,\partial_xv\,\mathrm{d}x+\int_0^1\left[a_6\omega\theta+\hat C\Lambda_\alpha\theta+a_5\Lambda_\alpha\partial_xu\right]v\,\mathrm{d}x=\int_0^1Qv\,\mathrm{d}x", "weak2")
P("On an element of length $l_e$ with the layer coefficients, the consistent mass matrix, the stiffness matrix, the thermoelastic coupling matrices and the mass matrix of the perfusion term are")
EQ(r"\mathbf{M}_e=\frac{l_e}{6}\left[2,\ 1;\ 1,\ 2\right],\qquad \mathbf{K}_e=\frac{1}{l_e}\left[1,\ -1;\ -1,\ 1\right]", "elem1")
EQ(r"\mathbf{G}_e=\frac{a_9}{2}\left[-1,\ -1;\ 1,\ 1\right],\qquad \mathbf{B}_e=\frac{a_5}{2}\left[-1,\ 1;\ -1,\ 1\right]", "elem2")
P("where the matrices are written row by row; $\\mathbf{G}_e$ is the coupling of the temperature into the momentum equation and $\\mathbf{B}_e$ is the coupling of the displacement into the energy equation. The perfusion term uses $a_6\\omega_e\\mathbf{M}_e$, where $\\omega_e$ is evaluated at the element mean temperature. The semi-discrete system for the nodal vectors $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\boldsymbol\\theta$ is")
EQ(r"\rho\mathbf{M}\,\partial_t^2\mathbf{u}+\left(1+\tau_1\partial_t\right)\left(a_8\mathbf{K}\mathbf{u}-\mathbf{G}\boldsymbol\theta\right)=\mathbf{0}", "semi1")
EQ(r"\hat C\mathbf{M}\,\Lambda_\alpha\boldsymbol\theta+\mathbf{B}\,\Lambda_\alpha\mathbf{u}+\left[\hat\kappa\mathbf{K}(\boldsymbol\theta)+a_6\omega\mathbf{M}\right]\boldsymbol\theta=\mathbf{q}(t)", "semi2")
P("where the coefficient matrices are assembled element by element with the coefficients of the layer to which the element belongs. The load vector of the laser source uses Simpson's rule on each element, which is exact for the quadratic variation of the integrand over the very small elements used here,")
EQ(r"q_{e,1}=\frac{a_7f(t)\,l_e}{6}\left[g(x_a)+2g(x_m)\right],\qquad q_{e,2}=\frac{a_7f(t)\,l_e}{6}\left[2g(x_m)+g(x_b)\right]", "load")
H2("3.3 Convolution quadrature in time")
P("All time derivatives, integer and fractional, are approximated by one family of formulas. For a function $z$ with $z(0)=0$ and with the values $z_n=z(t_n)$ at $t_n=n\\Delta t$, the derivative of order $p$ is approximated by a discrete convolution,")
EQ(r"D_t^{p}z\,(t_n)\approx\sum_{j=0}^{n}\omega_j^{(p)}\,z_{n-j}", "cq")
P("where the weights are the coefficients of the power series of the $p$-th power of the generating function of the second-order backward differentiation formula (BDF2):")
EQ(r"\sum_{j=0}^{\infty}\omega_j^{(p)}\zeta^{j}=\left(\frac{3-4\zeta+\zeta^{2}}{2\Delta t}\right)^{p}", "gen")
P("Because the generating function is rational, its logarithmic derivative gives a three-term recurrence for the coefficients $f_j=\\Delta t^{p}\\omega_j^{(p)}$, which is exact in exact arithmetic and stable in practice,")
EQ(r"f_0=\left(\frac32\right)^{p},\qquad 3(k+1)f_{k+1}=4(k-p)f_k+(2p-k+1)f_{k-1}", "rec")
P("For $p=1$ the weights are the BDF2 coefficients $(3,-4,1)/(2\\Delta t)$, for $p=2$ they are the five coefficients of the squared operator, and for $p=1+\\alpha$ they are infinite in number and form the memory of the fractional derivative. For integer $p$ the weights vanish exactly for $j>2p$ and are set to zero. "
  "Convolution quadrature based on a linear multistep method preserves the order of the method for smooth data, and it is A-stable and strongly damped for BDF2, which is why it was selected in preference to the trapezoidal rule whose weights alternate in sign and which rings after a step in the boundary data. "
  "The assumption $z(0)=\\partial_tz(0)=0$ is satisfied in the interior of the slab, and the initial state of the surface node is treated in Eq. (@bcnode@) below. By Eq. (@equiv@) the fractional term needs only the order $1+\\alpha$ weights applied to the field itself. The weights of the three operators that occur are")
EQ(r"c_j^{R}=\omega_j^{(2)},\qquad c_j^{1}=\delta_{j0}+\tau_1\omega_j^{(1)},\qquad c_j^{E}=\omega_j^{(1)}+\tau_0^{\alpha}\,\omega_j^{(1+\alpha)}", "cweights")
P("so that the three operators are $\\partial_t^2$, $1+\\tau_1\\partial_t$ and $\\Lambda_\\alpha$. At the time level $n$ the unknown vector is $\\mathbf{X}_n=(\\mathbf{u}_n,\\boldsymbol\\theta_n)$, with the nodal unknowns interleaved so that the matrix has a half bandwidth of three. The history of the past levels enters only through five vectors,")
EQ(r"\mathbf{h}_n^{R}=\sum_{j=1}^{n}c_j^{R}\mathbf{u}_{n-j},\quad \mathbf{h}_n^{1}=\sum_{j=1}^{n}c_j^{1}\mathbf{u}_{n-j},\quad \mathbf{k}_n^{1}=\sum_{j=1}^{n}c_j^{1}\boldsymbol\theta_{n-j}", "hist1")
EQ(r"\mathbf{h}_n^{E}=\sum_{j=1}^{n}c_j^{E}\mathbf{u}_{n-j},\qquad \mathbf{k}_n^{E}=\sum_{j=1}^{n}c_j^{E}\boldsymbol\theta_{n-j}", "hist2")
P("and the fully discrete equations of the level $n$ become a block system for $\\mathbf{X}_n$:")
EQ(r"c_0^{R}\rho\mathbf{M}\mathbf{u}_n+c_0^{1}\left(a_8\mathbf{K}\mathbf{u}_n-\mathbf{G}\boldsymbol\theta_n\right)=-\rho\mathbf{M}\mathbf{h}_n^{R}-a_8\mathbf{K}\mathbf{h}_n^{1}+\mathbf{G}\mathbf{k}_n^{1}", "disc1")
EQ(r"c_0^{E}\left(\hat C\mathbf{M}\boldsymbol\theta_n+\mathbf{B}\mathbf{u}_n\right)+\left[\hat\kappa\mathbf{K}+a_6\omega\mathbf{M}\right]\boldsymbol\theta_n=\mathbf{q}_n-\hat C\mathbf{M}\mathbf{k}_n^{E}-\mathbf{B}\mathbf{h}_n^{E}", "disc2")
P("The weight $c_0^{R}=\\omega_0^{(2)}=(3/2)^2/\\Delta t^2$ and the other weights with $j=0$ are the diagonal coefficients of the system. The stress is obtained afterwards from the element stress before the strain-rate operator, $S_e=a_8\\,\\partial_xu-a_9\\bar\\theta_e$ with $\\bar\\theta_e$ the element mean temperature, by the same convolution:")
EQ(r"\Sigma_{e,n}=\sum_{j=0}^{n}c_j^{1}S_{e,n-j}", "sigpost")
P("The surface temperature $\\theta_0H(t)$ is a step. The convolution quadrature interprets the node value at $t=0$ as part of the history, and the step is centred on $t=0$ when the value $\\theta_0/2$ is used for $H(0)$. For the smooth-ramp tests the boundary data are given by the analytic ramp:")
EQ(r"\theta_B(t_0)=\tfrac12\theta_0,\quad \theta_B(t_n)=\theta_0\ (n\ge1);\qquad \theta_B(t)=\theta_0\left(1-e^{-t/\tau_b}\right)\ \text{for the ramp}", "bcnode")
P("Two properties of this time discretisation are relevant to the accuracy statements of Section 4. The convolution quadrature based on BDF2 has order two for data that are smooth and compatible with the zero initial state, which means that the field and its first time derivative vanish at $t=0$ in a sufficiently regular way. The step in the surface temperature violates this condition at the surface, and the observed first-order behaviour is the known consequence of a missing starting correction. Corrections that restore the second order exist, but they require the expansion of the solution near $t=0$ in powers of $t^{\\beta}$ with exponents that depend on the layered problem and on $\\alpha$, and they were not added. The second property is stability: the BDF2 generating function is A-stable, so that no restriction links the time step to the mesh size, which is the reason why steps much larger than the transit time of a front across one element can be used.")
P("The cost per time step is proportional to $n\\,N_{\\mathrm{nodes}}$ for the history sums and to $N_{\\mathrm{nodes}}$ for the banded solve, so that the total cost grows as")
EQ(r"\text{cost}\sim \mathrm{O}\!\left(n_{\mathrm{step}}^{2}N_{\mathrm{nodes}}\right)+\mathrm{O}\!\left(n_{\mathrm{step}}N_{\mathrm{nodes}}N_{\mathrm{it}}\right)", "cost")
P("which is acceptable for the few hundred steps used. For the linear model the matrix is constant and its banded LU factorisation is computed once.")
H2("3.4 Picard iteration for the nonlinear terms")
P("With temperature-dependent perfusion or conductivity the matrix in Eq. (@disc2@) depends on the unknown temperature. The coefficients are lagged at the previous iterate and evaluated at the element mean temperature, so that the nonlinear term is treated as a quasi-linear operator with a secant coefficient (a Picard iteration),")
EQ(r"\bar\theta_e^{(k)}=\tfrac12\left(\theta_{e}^{(k)}+\theta_{e+1}^{(k)}\right),\qquad \hat\kappa_e^{(k)}=\hat\kappa\left(1+\chi\bar\theta_e^{(k)}\right),\qquad \omega_e^{(k)}=\omega\!\left(\bar\theta_e^{(k)}\right)", "pic1")
EQ(r"\mathbf{A}\!\left(\boldsymbol\theta^{(k)}\right)\mathbf{X}_n^{(k+1)}=\mathbf{b}_n,\qquad \mathbf{X}_n^{(0)}:\ \boldsymbol\theta^{(0)}=2\boldsymbol\theta_{n-1}-\boldsymbol\theta_{n-2}", "pic2")
EQ(r"r_k=\max_i\left|\theta_i^{(k+1)}-\theta_i^{(k)}\right|<10^{-11}", "pic3")
P("The linear predictor in the starting guess is a usual device that reduces the number of iterations. A Newton iteration would add the derivative $\\omega'(\\theta)\\theta$ to the matrix and could converge faster. The Picard form was chosen because the mass-like perfusion term has a small coefficient in the physical setting and the lagged matrix remains symmetric positive in its thermal block. The convergence is monitored through the ratio of successive residuals,")
EQ(r"\varrho_k=\frac{r_{k+1}}{r_k}", "pic4")
P("and it is superlinear in the computations reported below, which indicates that the fixed point map is a strong contraction at the amplitudes used.")
H2("3.5 Error measures, diagnostics and the choice of method")
P("The accuracy is measured against the Laplace-domain reference by the relative discrete $L_2$ error over a set of $N_p=19$ points $x_m=0.05\\,m$ at a fixed time, for a field $z\\in\\{\\theta,u,\\Sigma\\}$, and the convergence order is computed from successive errors $E_k$ at steps $\\Delta_k$:")
EQ(r"E_z=\sqrt{\frac{\sum_{m=1}^{N_p}\left(z^{h}(x_m)-z^{\mathrm{ref}}(x_m)\right)^{2}}{\sum_{m=1}^{N_p}z^{\mathrm{ref}}(x_m)^{2}}},\qquad p_{\mathrm{obs}}=\frac{\ln\left(E_{k-1}/E_k\right)}{\ln\left(\Delta_{k-1}/\Delta_k\right)}", "errs")
P("Two diagnostics are used to describe the physics. The position of the leading edge of the temperature disturbance is defined by a threshold, and the severity of the thermomechanical loading at the tumour edge by the peak stress magnitude there,")
EQ(r"x_f(t)=\max\left\{x:\ |\theta(x,t)|>10^{-3}\right\},\qquad \Sigma_{\max}=\max_{0\le t\le t_{\mathrm{end}}}\left|\Sigma(h,t)\right|", "diag")
P("The nonlinear terms are verified independently by a steady-state computation. At large times the transient terms vanish, and the temperature solves a two-point boundary value problem that does not involve the time discretisation at all,")
EQ(r"\partial_x\left(\hat\kappa(1+\chi\theta)\,\partial_x\theta\right)=a_6\,\omega(\theta)\,\theta,\qquad \theta(0)=\theta_0,\ \ \theta(1)=0", "steady")
P("with continuity of $\\theta$ and of $\\hat\\kappa(1+\\chi\\theta)\\theta'$ at $x=h$. This problem was solved with a finite volume discretisation on 2000 cells and the same Picard iteration, in a separate code. "
  "The wavelet route of the source study would replace the finite element space and time discretisation by a Legendre wavelet basis in space and time with an operational matrix of the Caputo derivative and a nonlinear solve per time step. It was not implemented. In order to reach a validated result, a method for which an exact comparison solution was available and which could be coded in pure Python without numerical libraries was chosen. "
  "A wavelet code would need its own validation against the same Laplace-domain solution, and the Laplace-domain solution of this paper would serve that purpose without change.")
